the pandemic is of course dramatically  changing our everyday lives as we  continue our series on the new normal we  look at how those changes will affect  the way we work socialize learn and vote  this week many voters in two states cast  their ballots by mail at O'Keefe shows  us how the push to make voting safer  while also safeguarding election  integrity the coronavirus means the days  of long lines of voters and libraries or  gymnasiums could be over at least  temporarily as states decide instead to  let more people vote by mail this is not  what any of us had envisioned months ago  for how we wanted to run this election  voters in Ohio in parts of Maryland  headed to the polls on Tuesday or drop  their ballot in the mail officials made  the change as they tried to balance  public health with participatory  democracy I have two main priorities  keeping Marylanders safe and protecting  their constitutional right to vote polls  show a majority of Americans now support  expanding the vote by mail option we  think it's a health issue at this point  the idea I think that mail-in voting is  a terrible thing  the corona virus has completely  scrambled the 2020 campaign calendar  only three states and the District of  Columbia are holding primaries is  scheduled every other state has either  postponed contests or expanded to a vote  by mail system and this week New York  for the first time canceled its  presidential primary set for June I've  seen lines of people on television  voting in other states as is totally  nonsensical a recent nonpartisan study  estimates it cost as much as 1.4 billion  dollars to ensure all voters have access  to mail-in ballots by November  proponents point to what happened with  the Wisconsin primary this month to  justify potential costs officials this  week said at least 52 people who voted  or worked the polls on April 7th have  tested positive for coronavirus it's  unclear how they got sick but the cases  confirm the fears of those who wanted to  delay the primary we could have moved to  an all-male in elec  and at the same time we could have  protected the integrity of the election  for CBS this morning I met O'Keefe in  Washington
 

